What this licence covers, and what it does not
==============================================

The MIT licence above covers this project's own work: the Python code, the scripts, the
app's own mark (konus/assets/konus.svg and .ico) and the documentation.

The first copyright line is the project this one grew out of — most of what is here is its
code, and MIT asks for that notice to be kept in copies. The second covers the work done on
top of it since. Both are under the same terms.

It does not cover these, which keep their own licences:

  vendor/                    python-vlc, LGPL-2.1-or-later (full text next to it,
                             in vendor/COPYING.python-vlc)
  konus/assets/fonts/        Inter, SIL Open Font License 1.1 (see OFL.txt there)
  konus/assets/icons.json    the interface's icon set: "VLC Redesign" by Juxtopposed,
                             CC BY 4.0 — see the credits in README.md
  licenses/GPL-3.0.txt       the GNU GPLv3's own text, as published by the FSF

Nothing that is fetched or installed rather than shipped (PyQt5, mutagen, the libVLC
runtime in runtime/) is licensed here at all.

Running this program means running it with PyQt5 (GPLv3) and mutagen (GPLv2-or-later), so
a *build* of it — a bundled .exe, or the folder handed to somebody else — is a GPLv3 work as a
whole and has to be distributed as one: with its source, with the licence text
(licenses/GPL-3.0.txt), and with a note of what was changed. MIT is compatible with the
GPLv3, so nothing here needs relicensing for that; it only means the combined work cannot
be distributed under anything more restrictive.
